          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "For newly diagnosed high blood pressure, the first-line treatment depends on the stage of hypertension and the presence of other health conditions, according to consensus guidance from a major cardiology society (from `guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment`).\n\n*   **For Stage 2 hypertension** (systolic blood pressure 140 mm Hg or higher, or diastolic 90 mm Hg or higher), drug treatment is recommended alongside lifestyle measures, usually starting with two medications.\n*   **For Stage 1 hypertension with established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk at or above 10 percent**, drug treatment is recommended alongside lifestyle measures.\n*   **For Stage 1 hypertension without these conditions**, lifestyle measures are recommended first, with reassessment after three to six months before considering drug treatment.\n\nThe first-line drug classes for most adults without other compelling indications are thiazide-type diuretics, angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs), and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers. ACE inhibitors and ARBs should not be combined (from `guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment`).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
